theory.intro:
An area conversion uses the length conversion factor **squared**. A metre is $100$ cm long, so a square metre is $100$ cm by $100$ cm, which is $10\,000$ square centimetres.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[fill=blue!15] (0,0) rectangle (5.2,5.2);
\draw[fill=blue!45] (0,0) rectangle (0.65,0.65);
\node[right] at (0.8,0.32) {$1$ small unit$^{2}$};
\node[below] at (2.6,-0.15) {$1$ big unit $=k$ small units};
\node[rotate=90] at (-0.9,2.6) {$1$ big unit $=k$ small units};
\node at (2.6,2.9) {$1$ big unit$^{2}$};
\node at (2.6,2.3) {$=k^{2}$ small units$^{2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
\draw[step=0.4,gray!50,very thin] (0,0) grid (4,4);
\draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (4,4);
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,amplitude=4pt}] (0,4.12)--(0.4,4.12) node[midway,above,font=\scriptsize] {$1\text{ mm}$};
\node[below] at (2,-0.1) {$1\text{ cm}$};
\node[rotate=90] at (-0.45,2) {$1\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The large square has area $1\text{ cm}^2$ and each small square has area $1\text{ mm}^2$.
How many $\text{mm}^2$ are there in $1\text{ cm}^2$?

foundation.solution_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
\fill[gray!25] (0,0) rectangle (4,0.4);
\draw[step=0.4,gray!50,very thin] (0,0) grid (4,4);
\draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (4,4);
\node[right] at (4.15,0.2) {$10$ squares};
\node[rotate=90] at (-0.45,2) {$10$ rows};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$10 \times 10$
$= 100\text{ mm}^2$

